# Digital Safety, Privacy, and Your Digital Footprint

> Every click leaves a trace: posts you write, cookies you collect,
> records others keep about you. This lesson turns cybersecurity into
> daily habits, closing with India's IT Act 2000 and CERT-In.

## 1. Your Digital Footprint: Active and Passive

Your **digital footprint** is the trail of data you leave across the
internet. It has two halves, and the second is easy to forget.

@diagram tree id="digital-footprint-map" layout="tree" aria="Digital footprint split into active, passive, and third-party traces"
  "Digital footprint" -> "Active: posts, comments, uploads"
  "Digital footprint" -> "Passive: cookies, location pings, device fingerprints"
  "Digital footprint" -> "Third party: tags, mentions, breached databases"
@/diagram

| Trace | Who creates it | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Active | you, deliberately | photos, tweets, form submissions |
| Passive | websites, silently | tracking cookies, IP logs, browsing history |
| Third party | others | a friend tags you, a leaked password list includes you |

[definition]
Data minimisation: share the least data needed for the task, with the
fewest parties, for the shortest time. The footprint you never create
cannot leak, be profiled, or be sold.
[/definition]

[warning]
The internet has a long memory. Deleted posts are cached, screenshotted,
and archived; assume anything you publish could someday be read by an
employer, an admission committee, or a stranger with bad intentions.
[/warning]

## 2. Password Hygiene: Length, Uniqueness, Randomness

Three rules carry most of the weight: make passwords **long**,
**unique per site**, and **unpredictable**. A four-word passphrase
like *river-lantern-cactus-quilt* beats *P@ssw0rd!*, because every
extra character multiplies the search space astronomically.

@chart bar title="Illustrative offline crack time by password length (seconds, log-like scale)" labels:
8 characters
10 characters
12 characters
14 characters

data:
2200
8400000
32000000000
120000000000000
@/chart

The chart assumes a random mix of letters, digits, and symbols
against a fast hash at a hundred billion guesses per second — the
jump from hours to millions of years is the whole argument for length.

| Common mistake | Why it fails | Do this instead |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Password123 patterns | first thing crackers try | random passphrase |
| Reuse across sites | one breach opens every account | unique per site |
| Personal details | birthdays are public data | unguessable words |
| Short but complex | Tr0ub4dor! is crackable | length over symbol tricks |

[tip]
Exam shortcut: when options differ on length versus complexity, pick
length and uniqueness. Complexity rules without length produce
"P@ssw0rd", which every cracking dictionary already contains.
[/tip]

## 3. Password Managers

A **password manager** generates and stores strong unique passwords
behind one strong **master password** and encryption. You memorise a
single passphrase; the vault remembers hundreds.

| Benefit | Explanation |
| --- | --- |
| Unique everywhere | a breach at one site stays at that site |
| Strong by default | generated random strings you never need to type |
| Autofill with checks | managers refuse to fill on lookalike phishing domains |
| Sync and recovery | vaults sync encrypted across your devices |

Choose a reputable manager, protect the master password with MFA, and
export an encrypted backup. The vault becomes the single crown jewel —
so its master password must be long, unshared, and never reused.

## 4. MFA: Something You Know, Have, or Are

**Multi-factor authentication (MFA)** demands proof from two or more
different factor families, so a stolen password alone is useless.

@diagram tree id="authenticator-factors" layout="tree" aria="Authentication factors grouped into knowledge, possession, and inherence"
  "Authentication factors" -> "Knowledge: passwords, PINs"
  "Authentication factors" -> "Possession: OTP apps, SMS codes, security keys"
  "Authentication factors" -> "Inherence: fingerprint, face, voice"
@/diagram

Two factors from the *same* family do not count as MFA — a password
plus a security question is still just knowledge twice.

| Method | Strength | Main weakness |
| --- | --- | --- |
| SMS one-time codes | better than nothing | SIM-swap attacks, phishing |
| Authenticator apps | strong, offline-capable | device loss, backup codes |
| Push approval | convenient | prompt fatigue attacks |
| Hardware security keys | strongest, phishing-resistant | cost, need a spare |

[warning]
Prompt fatigue is real: an attacker who knows your password spams
login approvals at 2 a.m. hoping you tap "yes" to stop the noise.
Never approve a prompt you did not trigger; use number matching or a
security key where offered.
[/warning]

## 5. Safe Browsing Habits

Small habits block most web-delivered threats:

- Check that the address is exactly right — **typosquatting** sites
  live at rnicrosoft.com and paypa1.com.
- HTTPS matters for login pages, but a padlock on a scam site just
  means the scam is encrypted.
- Download apps and files only from official stores and vendors.
- Keep browsers, extensions, and the OS updated — exploits chase
  unpatched software.
- On public Wi-Fi, prefer your phone's hotspot or a VPN; never log in
  to banking over open networks without a tunnel.
- Log out of shared computers, and decline "remember me" on devices
  that are not yours.

[info title="Quizzes and Facebook-questions"]
"Which movie character is your first pet?" is a password-reset
question being harvested in public. Anything used as a security
answer — school, birthplace, first car — should never be answered
truthfully in public posts.
[/info]

## 6. Recognising Phishing in Daily Life

Phishing arrives by email, SMS, WhatsApp, and calls; the tells are
constant across channels.

| Sign | What you might see |
| --- | --- |
| Urgency and threats | "account closed in 24 hours" |
| Authority pressure | "the CEO needs this done now" |
| Mismatched sender | display name says bank, domain says mail.ru |
| Generic greeting | "Dear customer" instead of your name |
| Payload pressure | unexpected attachment or short-link |
| Payment or OTP demand | no genuine service asks for your OTP |

[example]
A message claims your electricity will be cut tonight and asks you to
pay a small amount on a linked page. The link resolves to a fresh
domain registered last week. Verify through the official app or the
number printed on your bill — never through the message itself.
[/example]

If you already clicked: disconnect, change the password from a clean
device, enable MFA, and report it. Speed limits the damage more than
embarrassment limits the reporting.

## 7. Managing Your Footprint

Footprint management is periodic hygiene, not a one-time cleanup:

| Action | Frequency |
| --- | --- |
| Search your own name and review results | every few months |
| Audit app permissions and revoke unused ones | yearly |
| Tighten social media privacy to friends-only | after each platform change |
| Delete or deactivate dormant accounts | as noticed |
| Review what data brokers and old forums hold | yearly |

[important]
Before posting, run the three-question test: would I be comfortable if
my employer, my family, and a stranger all saw this? Assume every
post is permanent and public by default.
[/important]

## 8. India's Legal Framework: IT Act 2000 and CERT-In

India's baseline cyber law is the **Information Technology Act,
2000**, amended in 2008. It gives legal recognition to electronic
records and signatures and defines offences with penalties.

| Provision | Offence | Punishment outline |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Section 43 | unauthorised access, data damage | compensation to the victim |
| Section 65 | tampering with computer source code | up to 3 years imprisonment and fine |
| Section 66 | computer-related offences | up to 3 years and/or fine |
| Section 66C | identity theft | up to 3 years and fine |
| Section 66D | cheating by personation online | up to 3 years and fine |
| Section 67 | publishing obscene material | up to 3 years first conviction and fine |

[info title="CERT-In"]
CERT-In, constituted in 2004 under Section 70B of the IT Act, is
India's national cyber incident response agency: it issues alerts and
advisories, coordinates incident response, and — under its 2022
directions — requires service providers to report specified cyber
incidents within six hours. Citizens can report cybercrime at
cybercrime.gov.in or the 1930 helpline for financial fraud.
[/info]

The **Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023** adds a consent-based
regime for how organisations handle personal data, complementing the
IT Act's **SPDI Rules, 2011**, which require reasonable security
practices for sensitive personal data.

## 9. Quick Revision

| Concept | Remember this |
| --- | --- |
| Digital footprint | active posts plus passive tracking plus third-party traces |
| Password rules | long, unique, random — passphrase beats P@ssw0rd |
| Password manager | one strong master password, many strong unique ones |
| MFA families | knowledge, possession, inherence — two different ones |
| Strongest 2FA | hardware security key, phishing-resistant |
| SIM swap | why SMS codes are the weakest factor |
| Typosquatting | lookalike domains like paypa1.com |
| Data minimisation | least data, fewest parties, shortest time |
| IT Act 2000 | India's cyber law; 66C identity theft, 66D online cheating |
| CERT-In | national incident response; 6-hour reporting direction |
